title: Why bddr
section: Introduction
desc: What bddr is, who it is for, and what it adds to Gherkin specs: orchestration, resilience, plugins and reports.
lede: bddr is a test runner for automation suites written as Gherkin specs. It keeps the readable feature files and adds the control that large UI and API suites need: what runs in parallel, what must run in order, and what to do when a test fails.
---
<h2>What bddr is</h2>
<p>You write scenarios in <code>.feature</code> files and the steps behind them in TypeScript. bddr finds the scenarios, builds a schedule, runs them on workers, retries what should be retried and writes the reports.</p>
<p>It is an automation framework with BDD-style specs, not a methodology. Use the specs to share what a test does with people who do not read code. Use the runner to get a fast, stable suite.</p>
<aside class="callout note"><p class="callout-title">Not tied to a browser library</p><p>bddr core depends on no browser or HTTP library. Playwright, Selenium and axios are plugins that your project installs. See <a href="plugins.html">How plugins work</a>.</p></aside>

<h2>What you get</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>You need to</th><th>bddr gives you</th><th>Read</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Run independent tests side by side</td><td><code>workers</code> and an optional process pool: one Node.js process per worker.</td><td><a href="process-pool.html">Worker processes</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Keep a journey in order inside a parallel run</td><td><code>@serial</code> on a feature. Other features keep running.</td><td><a href="serial-features.html">Serial features</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Run setup first, reports last</td><td>Groups with <code>dependsOn</code>, plus <code>@dependsOn</code> between scenarios.</td><td><a href="groups.html">Groups</a>, <a href="dependencies.html">Dependencies</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Stop two tests from using the same database at once</td><td><code>@lock:name</code>, <code>@exclusive</code> and <code>@priority:n</code>.</td><td><a href="locks.html">Locks and priority</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Survive a flaky environment without hiding it</td><td>Scenario, group and run retries. A pass after a failure is reported as flaky. <code>@quarantine</code> keeps a known-bad test visible without failing the build.</td><td><a href="retries.html">Retries and flaky tests</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Split a suite across CI machines</td><td><code>--shard i/n</code>, balanced by recorded duration.</td><td><a href="sharding.html">Sharding in CI</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Drive test data from files</td><td><code>@data:</code> with CSV, TSV, JSON, YAML and Excel.</td><td><a href="data-files.html">Data files</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Use a real browser or API client</td><td>Plugins that expose the full Playwright, Selenium or axios API as fixtures.</td><td><a href="playwright.html">Playwright</a>, <a href="selenium.html">Selenium</a>, <a href="axios.html">axios</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Show results to a team</td><td>A single-file HTML report with a timeline, dependency graph and history. JUnit XML, Cucumber JSON and an NDJSON event stream.</td><td><a href="reporters.html">Reporters</a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>One run, parallel and serial together</h2>
<p>Most suites mix work that can run side by side with work that cannot. A login test and a catalogue test are independent. A checkout journey is not: add to basket, pay, see the receipt. bddr plans both in one run, so you do not split the suite into separate jobs.</p>
<figure class="shot"><img src="../assets/img/why-schedule.png" alt="A run schedule with four worker lanes: setup runs first while the other workers wait, then checkout runs in parallel on three workers while payments runs on the fourth holding a lock, then reports run last." width="2400" height="1120" loading="lazy"></figure>

<h2>How it is built</h2>
<p>Four small packages, a plan that is plain data, and plugins for browsers and API clients. <a href="architecture.html">Architecture</a> shows how they fit together.</p>

<h2>Who it is for</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Teams with a UI and API suite that has outgrown one sequential run.</li>
  <li>QA engineers who write specs and want developers to share the step code.</li>
  <li>Teams moving from Cucumber.js who want orchestration without writing their own scripts. See <a href="from-cucumber.html">Coming from Cucumber.js</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When another tool fits better</h2>
<ul>
  <li>You write unit tests. Use your language's unit test runner.</li>
  <li>You only need a handful of browser tests and nobody reads the specs. A plain Playwright Test project is simpler.</li>
  <li>You need a stable 1.0 today. bddr is at version 0.1 and is not on npm yet. You build it from the repository. See <a href="getting-started.html">Getting started</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Current state</h2>
<p>The TypeScript runtime, CLI, reporters and the Playwright, Selenium and axios plugins are working. A Java port is planned and will use the same configuration, event stream and conformance tests. Planned work, such as a VS Code extension, is listed in <code>docs/STATUS.md</code> in the repository.</p>
